  "content": {
    "id": "p7-q15-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "15",
    "prompt": "Consider the globe of the earth with a rope wrapped tightly around the equator. Now lengthen the rope by exactly $1$ metre. Finally, lift this (now loose) rope a uniform distance above the globe. Will a mouse fit under the rope?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": {
        "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
        "version": 1,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "id": "p7-q15-short-result",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Yes. The clearance is ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\text{ m}\\approx15.9\\text{ cm}",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ".",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p7-q15-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Subtract the original circumference from the new circumference to find the increase in radius.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Let $r$ m be Earth's radius, $R$ m the new rope radius and $C$ m the original rope length.\n$$\\begin{align*}C&=2\\pi r\\\\C+1&=2\\pi R.\\end{align*}$$\nSubtracting the first equation from the second gives\n$$\\begin{align*}1&=2\\pi(R-r)\\\\R-r&=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\text{ m}\\\\R-r&\\approx0.159\\text{ m}\\\\R-r&\\approx15.9\\text{ cm}.\\end{align*}$$\nYes, a mouse can fit beneath a rope lifted about $15.9$ cm above the globe.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 84
  },
